A||||10||ITA_FIERO||Rev Limiter||||1||
Z||000000||ITA_FIERO||01-27-2000||10:40 PM||||I have an 86 6 cyl that is soon to have a 5-speed Muncie/Getrag.  I know that the rev limiter in stock form is controlled by the EProm chip and that there are several aftermarket chips for the Fiero.  What I would really like to know is which aftermarket chips raise (or eliminate) the rev limit, and are these chips made specifically for certain years (ie Do I need to order a chip for the 88 to get rid of the rev limit).  Any help would be greatly appreciated.<BR>And for anyone who knows SCCA ITA rules, yes I'm aware that aftermarket chips are illegal.  This question is...um...for a friend.  Yea, that's it...a friend.<BR>(2 PS's First, I like this being a "junior" member!  It's been a really long time since I've been a "junior" anything.  Second, I really just want this chip for testing purposes, so if anyone knows a way around the rev limit without changing chips, I would love to hear how to save me some money.)<p>[This message has been edited by ITA_FIERO (edited 01-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||David Gamache||01-28-2000||12:42 AM||||I used to work for a speed shop and still could not answer this one positively. I would contact the manufacturers of ADS or Hyper chip or who ever. Most speed shops also have a number they can call for direct tec help. As far as I remember most chips only create the same effect as advancing the distributor and changing the springs and weights in the good old days on older cars. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||01-28-2000||01:59 AM||||You are probably doing some other things to your....uh....friends...engine but if you keep it stock there really isn't any more "umph" after 5500 rpms. Its grunt is in the low rpms (God bless it!)What do you hope to gain by eliminating the rev limiter? Are you going to go to stronger valve springs to eliminate floating? Or is your "Friend's" car going to the cam grind that doesn't kick in until 6000....hmmmmmmm..... your friend's would certainly not act like most Fieros on the SCCA circuit....<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-28-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||mwbackus||01-28-2000||02:06 AM||||The prom used in the V6 88's have the rev limiter disabled.  If you have one laying around you could try it, but I really doubt it would work.  Why would you want to remove the rev limiter anyway?  It could easily save you from a premature engine rebuild.<BR>I run a 6000 rpm limit module in the MSD box on my formula.  I really hate the idea of paying $$$ for a few missed gears.  <P>Still, if you have built your motor for it, it is quite normal to raise the rev limit to take advantage of the higher rpm capabilities realized from the enhancements.  There is a custom chip available that increases the rev limiter.  It is quite inexpensive (~$50) and it would be hard to identify without an ECM scanner.  For more info on this chip contact:<P>"S. Joe Wynman" &lt;Fierojo@bellatlantic.net&gt;<P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||86SE||01-28-2000||08:43 AM||||Does a rev limiter kill power to your engine when it reaches 6000RPM??  Does my auto 86 SE V-6 have a rev limiter that limits the revs to 6000??  im clueless............i learn something new everyday about Fieros!!||||reg||5
Z||000005||88 Fiero||01-28-2000||12:01 PM||||When I bought my '88 Formula.. The guy installed a cam and an aftermarket chip. He said that chip also eliminated the rev limiter and I can atest that results for I have tried it before. It's neat that I could rev my engine pass 6000 rpm's but I don't do it all the time...ti'll I build my V6||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||01-28-2000||01:05 PM||||I forget, does the rev limiter cut the fuel or ignition? <P>Are you still really going to sell your Fiero Gabe?||||reg||5
Z||000008||terryk||01-28-2000||04:05 PM||||if it's set, it's likely at 6012 on and 4008 of.<P>MPH limiter, if set and not all are, is 124 and 120 MPH.<P>Fuel is killed. Haven't looked it spark is.<P>This is on the Fiero, other methods are used in various cars.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 01-28-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||olympic||01-28-2000||04:07 PM||||   Most rev limiters work by cutting the spark to half the cylinders. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||ITA_FIERO||01-28-2000||05:54 PM||||You all have made some good points! The ITA rules require and EXTREMELY stock motor but I do have an Edelbrock header that breaths extremely well and I've "altered" some of the computer input(legally, of course) so it will pull up to 6K with little problems.  But to answer the "Why do I want to eliminate the rev limiter" questions, I really don't want to eliminate it!  I would, however, like to have it closer to 6200 or 6300, which I can set with the ignition (MSD 6AL), but I have to get past 6K first.  In head to head racing there are times when you have to ride a particular gear a fraction of a second longer than you normally would(and you can just plain screw up and miss a shift) and losing power for a second or two in turn 5 or 6 at Watkins Glen can really suck.  <BR>To mwbackus, I've heard the 88 chip has no rev limiter, why do you think it wouldn't work?<BR>To 86SE, yes your engine will essentially "die" at 6000 RPM.  I agree with olympic that MOST rev limiters cut the spark to some of the cylinders but the Fiero's computer cuts the fuel (presumably to protect the cylinder walls from being washed of oil and to prevent some funky detonations when spark is reintroduced).  The net effect is that you are a temporary sitting duck on a race track. <BR>To David, calling the manufacturers is a good idea and one I'll take if nobody here can help.<BR>Thanks All,  Bill||||reg||1
Z||000011||terryk||01-28-2000||07:47 PM||||The 88 chip I have is set to 4008 and 6013 (off/on). Manual trans.<P>I've seen an 88 set to max or 1.3M RPM/655k RPM. Strange, I know, but true.||||reg||1
